t up a number to repeat after Granite writes it. However, if the sequence has period 4, then Pomegranate writes every other term.\n\nWait, in the case when the period is even, say 4, Pomegranate writes terms2,4,6,8,... So if the sequence is 3,2,0,1,3,2,0,1..., then Pomegranate writes terms2 (difference2), term4 (difference0), term6 (difference2), etc. So in this case, the first time a number repeats under Pomegranate\'s control would be at step4 (0), which she writes. But the previous repetition is at step2 (2) by Pomegranate, but step2 and step6 are written by Pomegranate. However, the first repetition that ends the game is step3 and step7, which are both written by Pomegranate. So in this case, Pomegranate can control when the game ends by ensuring that when the sequence cycles back, she writes the repeated number.\n\nTherefore, in order for Pomegranate to have a winning strategy, she must be able to make sure that the period of the sequence is even, so that there exists a repetition of numbers, and since she alternates writing with Granite, she can ensure that when the cycle repeats, she is the one to write the repeated number. Therefore, for Pomegranate to have a winning strategy, the period of the sequence must be even. Thus, the problem reduces to finding all primes p < 10^5 such that for all c in {2,3,..., p-1}, the sequence defined by t_{n+1}=c t_n + d mod p has even period. However, since the linear function is invertible modulo p, the period of the aperiodic sequence (i.e., not considering the constant term) is equal to the multiplicative order of c modulo p, which for invertible c divides p-1. If the multiplicative order is a power of 2, then the period is even. Otherwise, it might be odd.\n\nBut with the addition of the constant term d, the situation is more complicated. However, if we can choose d such that for any c, the mapping f(t)=c t + d mod p is such that the recurrence relation forces even periods. Wait, is there a way to choose d such that regardless of c, the equation f^k(x) ≡x mod p has even k?\n\nAlternatively, maybe choosing d=1. But in general, it\'s unclear. Let me think of another approach. Suppose that for each c, Pomegranate can choose d and x such that the equation f^k(x)≡x mod p has an even minimal k. This would require that for each c, the mapping f(t)=c t +d mod p has a minimal period that is even. For linear functions modulo a prime, it is known that for quadratic non-residues, the minimal period is p−1 (which is even), but wait, is that the case?\n\nWait, if c is a quadratic non-residue, then the multiplicative order of c modulo p is even. Therefore, the minimal period would be even. If c is a quadratic residue, then the multiplicative order is not necessarily even. For example, c=1 has order 1. However, c=1 is not allowed here. Therefore, if Pomegranate can make sure that c is always a quadratic非residue, then periods would be even. But in this problem, Granite can choose any c in {2,3,..., p−1}. Therefore, if p is such that all elements in {2,3,..., p−1} are quadratic non-residues, then the periods would be even. However, this is impossible, because in a field of order p, exactly half of the elements are quadratic residues and half are non-residues (excluding 0). So if p is odd, then {2,3,..., p−1} contains (p−3)/2 quadratic residues and (p−3)/2 quadratic non-residues if p is odd. Therefore, there are both residues and non-residues. Therefore, for some c\'s, periods would be odd. Therefore, Pomegranate cannot choose d and x such that all possible c\'s lead to even periods. Thus, perhaps there is another way.\n\nWait, maybe if the constant term d is chosen such that the linear recurrence relation f(t) = c t +d mod p always leads to a period even when iterated. For example, if we can make the recurrence relation have even minimal period regardless of c.\n\nLet me think of the characteristic equation of the recurrence. The recurrence t_{n+1} = c t_n + d has the characteristic equation x = c x 