/c*(1 - c)/1 ??? Wait, perhaps this is more complicated.\n\nAlternatively, consider that t_0 is the initial term. Then t_1 = c t_0 + d, t_2 = c t_1 + d = c (c t_0 + d ) +d = c^2 t_0 + c d + d, and so on. So in general, t_n = c^n t_0 + d (c^{n-1} + c^{n-2} + ... +1 ). The sum c^{n-1} + ... +1 = (c^{n} -1)/(c -1). Therefore, t_n = c^n t_0 + d (c^n -1)/(c -1). When does t_n ≡ t_m mod p for some m >n?\n\nThat is, c^n t_0 + d (c^n -1)/(c -1) ≡ t_n ≡ c^m t_0 + d (c^m -1)/(c -1) mod p.\n\nTherefore, (c^n - c^m) t_0 + d (c^n - c^m )/(c -1) ≡0 mod p.\n\nFactoring out (c^n - c^m):\n\n(c^n - c^m) (t_0 + d/(c -1)) ≡0 mod p.\n\nSince c and p are coprime, c^n - c^m ≡0 mod p if and only if c^{m}(c^{n -m} -1 ) ≡0 mod p.\n\nAssuming p does not divide c, this implies c^{n -m} ≡1 mod p. Therefore, the minimal period divides p -1.\n\nThus, to have t_n ≡ t_m mod p, we require that either:\n\n1. c^{n -m} ≡ 1 mod p, or\n\n2. (t_0 + d/(c -1)) ≡0 mod p.\n\nCase 1: If c^{n -m} ≡1 mod p, then after some multiple of the multiplicative order of c mod p, we have repetition. So if the multiplicative order divides n -m, which implies that n -m is a multiple of the order. So the minimal period is the multiplicative order of c mod p.\n\nCase 2: If (t_0 + d/(c -1)) ≡0 mod p, then for any n, the term t_n would be ≡0 mod p. But this seems to require t_0 + d/(c -1) ≡0. Therefore, Pomegranate can choose t_0 = -d/(c -1). But t_0 is the initial value written by Granite, which Pomegranate does not control. Therefore, this is not under Pomegranate\'s control. Hence, Pomegranate cannot set t_0; she must choose d and x (which becomes t_0).\n\nTherefore, Pomegranate can only affect the second condition if she can choose d such that for all possible c and t_0, there exists an n where t_n ≡0 mod p. However, since t_n = c^n t_0 + d (c^n -1)/(c -1). If Pomegranate can ensure that for any c and t_0, this expression can be ≡0 mod p for some n. But this seems difficult, as t_0 is arbitrary.\n\nAlternatively, if Pomegranate can set up the equation such that t_n ≡ t_{n -k} mod p for some k >0, regardless of c.\n\nBut given that in order for two terms to be congruent modulo p, the necessary condition is that their difference is ≡0 mod p.\n\nAlternatively, let\'s think in terms of group theory. The function f(t) = c t + d is a bijection on the integers modulo p, so it induces a permutation on the residues mod p. The period of the permutation is the minimal positive integer m such that f^m(t) ≡t mod p for all t. Therefore, Pomegranate has a winning strategy if and only if for all c, the permutation induced by f(t)=c t +d has even period.\n\nTherefore, the problem reduces to finding all primes p < 10^5 such that every permutation of the form f(t)=c t +d mod p (with c ∈ {2,3,..., p-1}) has even period. Note that d varies as well, so Pomegranate can choose d for each c.\n\nTherefore, the key is to find p such that for any invertible c mod p and any d, the permutation f_c(t)=c t +d mod p has even周期.\n\nNote that when c ≡1 mod p, the permutation is f(t)=t +d, which has period 1. However, c is chosen from {2,...,p-1}, so c ≡1 mod p is forbidden. Therefore, all c considered are such that c ≡2,..., p-1 mod p.\n\nNow, for a permutation f_c(t) =c t +d mod p, the order of the permutation (i.e., the minimal m such that f_c^m(t) ≡t mod p for all t) is equal to the minimal m such that c^m t + d sum_{k=0}^{m-1} c^k ≡ t mod p for all t. That is, after m applications, we have:\n\nf_c^m(t) ≡c^m t + d \\sum_{k=0}^{m-1} c^k ≡ t mod p.\n\nRearranging, this requires:\n\nc^m t + d \\frac{c^m -1}{c -1} ≡t mod p.\n\nThus,\n\n(c^m -1)t + d \\frac{c^m -1}{c -1} ≡0 mod p.\n\nFactoring out (c^m -1):\n\n(c^m -1)(t + \\frac{d}{c -1}) ≡0 mod p.\n\nSince this must hold for all t, we must have:\n\n(c^m -1) ≡0 mod p and t + \\frac{d}{c -1} ≡0 mod p.\n\nBut since p does not divide c -1 (as c ∈ {2,...,p -1}), the term \\frac{d}{c -1} is not an integer modulo p unless d ≡0. However, d is chosen by Pomegranate, so she can